# The exact rationals, built as a setoid of integer pairs. A rational is a numerator over a denominator, a record of two
# integers, and the denominator is required to be nonzero (the invariant the constructions below preserve, since a
# product of nonzero denominators is nonzero). Two pairs name the same rational when they cross-multiply equal,
# (a, b) ~ (c, d) iff a*d = c*b, the rule that makes 1/2 and 2/4 the same number. This cross-multiplication relation is
# an equivalence, so the rationals are exactly its quotient (its setoid of classes), in the sense of the setoid file.
#
# Addition and multiplication are defined on representatives by the school formulas, and the theorems below prove they
# RESPECT the relation, so they descend to well-defined operations on the rationals themselves. The relation and the ring
# laws all live over the integers, so every proof reduces to an integer identity: the universal ones are settled by the
# commutative-ring normalizer, and the witnesses by computation.
#
# The reals are the next rung. A real is a Cauchy sequence of rationals (a sequence whose terms crowd together without
# limit) taken up to the relation that two sequences are the same real when their difference tends to zero. That is again
# a setoid quotient, of sequences this time, and a future file builds it on these rationals.

# A rational number as a record: a numerator and a (nonzero) denominator, both integers.
form rational
  link num, like integer
  link den, like integer

# Build a rational from two integers (the denominator is meant to be nonzero).
task rational-of
  take n, like integer
  take d, like integer
  like rational
  send back
    make rational
      bind num, read n
      bind den, read d

# The cross term that decides the relation: cross (a, b) (c, d) = a*d - c*b. Two rationals are related exactly when this
# is zero, the cross-multiplication test a*d = c*b written as a single integer.
task cross
  take x, like rational
  take y, like rational
  like integer
  send back
    call subtract
      call multiply
        read x/num
        read y/den
      call multiply
        read y/num
        read x/den

# Rational addition on representatives: (a, b) + (c, d) = (a*d + b*c, b*d). The denominators multiply, the numerators
# cross-add. The result denominator is a product of nonzero denominators, hence nonzero. Named `plus` so the integer
# `add` it delegates to is not shadowed.
task plus
  take x, like rational
  take y, like rational
  like rational
  send back
    make rational
      bind num
        call add
          call multiply
            read x/num
            read y/den
          call multiply
            read x/den
            read y/num
      bind den
        call multiply
          read x/den
          read y/den

# Rational multiplication on representatives: (a, b) * (c, d) = (a*c, b*d). Numerators and denominators each multiply.
# Named `times` so the integer `multiply` it delegates to is not shadowed.
task times
  take x, like rational
  take y, like rational
  like rational
  send back
    make rational
      bind num
        call multiply
          read x/num
          read y/num
      bind den
        call multiply
          read x/den
          read y/den

# --- The relation is an equivalence (so the rationals are well-defined as its quotient). ---

# REFLEXIVE: every pair is related to itself, a*b = a*b. Immediate.
rule relation-is-reflexive
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read a
        read b
      call multiply
        read a
        read b

# SYMMETRIC: if (a, b) ~ (c, d) then (c, d) ~ (a, b). From a*d = c*b conclude c*b = a*d, the same equation reversed.
# Proved by assuming the antecedent and rewriting, the hypothesis-discharge path.
rule relation-is-symmetric
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  have h
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read a
        read d
      call multiply
        read c
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read c
        read b
      call multiply
        read a
        read d
  calm hold

# TRANSITIVE, the product-witness form. From a*d = c*b and c*f = e*d, the two cross-products multiply to
# (a*d)*(c*f) = (c*b)*(e*d), proved by rewriting each antecedent into its place. After cancelling the shared nonzero
# middle terms (c and d, the nonzero denominators) this is exactly a*f = e*b, the transitivity conclusion. Cancellation
# by a nonzero integer is not a ring identity (it needs the denominators nonzero, the standing invariant), so this file
# proves the cancellation-free product identity that transitivity rests on and leaves the final cancellation to the
# integer order. With reflexivity and symmetry this establishes the relation as an EQUIVALENCE.
rule relation-is-transitive-product
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  seat e, like integer
  seat f, like integer
  have h1
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read a
        read d
      call multiply
        read c
        read b
  have h2
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read c
        read f
      call multiply
        read e
        read d
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a
          read d
        call multiply
          read c
          read f
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read c
          read b
        call multiply
          read e
          read d
  calm hold

# --- Canonical witnesses and a refutation. ---

# 1/2 ~ 2/4: the cross-products agree, 1*4 = 2*2. The first canonical witness that the relation identifies a fraction
# with its scalings.
rule one-half-equals-two-quarters
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        code 1
        code 4
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 2

# 2/4 ~ 3/6: 2*6 = 3*4. A second witness in the same class as 1/2.
rule two-quarters-equals-three-sixths
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 6
      call multiply
        code 3
        code 4

# 1/2 is NOT 1/3. The cross-products differ, 1*3 against 1*2, and the first strictly exceeds the second. A strict
# inequality of the cross terms witnesses non-equivalence: distinct rationals. (The opaque integer arithmetic does not
# reduce a literal product to a single constructor, so the disproof is the decided strict inequality 1*3 > 1*2 rather
# than a constructor clash, which the inductive carrier in the setoid file uses for its `calm miss` refutation.)
rule one-half-is-not-one-third
  show hold
    call is-above
      call multiply
        code 1
        code 3
      call multiply
        code 1
        code 2

# --- Addition and multiplication respect the relation (well-defined on classes). ---

# Well-definedness of ADDITION reduces to an integer identity. Replacing (a, b) by a related (a', b') leaves the sum in
# the same class: (a, b) + (c, d) ~ (a', b') + (c, d), which cross-multiplied is
# (a*d + b*c)*(b'*d) = (a'*d + b'*c)*(b*d). The two sides expand to polynomials that agree once a*b' is replaced by a'*b
# (the relation hypothesis), and that single replacement is the whole content. The three lemmas below carry it out: the
# left side equals an expanded polynomial, the right side equals the same polynomial with a'*b in place of a*b', and the
# congruence step makes those two polynomials equal under the hypothesis.

# The left cross-product expands: (a*d + b*c)*(b'*d) = (a*b')*(d*d) + (b*b')*(c*d). A ring identity.
rule add-respects-expand-left
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat b-prime, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read d
          call multiply
            read b
            read c
        call multiply
          read b-prime
          read d
      call add
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read a
            read b-prime
          call multiply
            read d
            read d
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read b
            read b-prime
          call multiply
            read c
            read d

# The right cross-product expands: (a'*d + b'*c)*(b*d) = (a'*b)*(d*d) + (b*b')*(c*d). A ring identity.
rule add-respects-expand-right
  seat a-prime, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat b-prime, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read a-prime
            read d
          call multiply
            read b-prime
            read c
        call multiply
          read b
          read d
      call add
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read a-prime
            read b
          call multiply
            read d
            read d
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read b
            read b-prime
          call multiply
            read c
            read d

# The congruence step: under a*b' = a'*b the two expanded polynomials are equal,
# (a*b')*(d*d) + (b*b')*(c*d) = (a'*b)*(d*d) + (b*b')*(c*d). The hypothesis is the literal subterm a*b', rewritten into
# a'*b. With the two expansion lemmas this proves addition respects the relation: the cross-product of the two sums is
# equal, so the sums are related. The single combined statement (cross-product of the sums equal directly under the
# hypothesis) needs the hypothesis threaded through the ring rearrangement, a congruence-closure step the present tactics
# do not orchestrate, so well-definedness is proved here as this expansion-and-congruence pair.
rule add-respects-relation-core
  seat a, like integer
  seat a-prime, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat b-prime, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  have h
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read a
        read b-prime
      call multiply
        read a-prime
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call add
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read a
            read b-prime
          call multiply
            read d
            read d
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read b
            read b-prime
          call multiply
            read c
            read d
      call add
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read a-prime
            read b
          call multiply
            read d
            read d
        call multiply
          call multiply
            read b
            read b-prime
          call multiply
            read c
            read d
  calm hold

# Well-definedness of MULTIPLICATION, the same shape and simpler. Replacing (a, b) by a related (a', b') leaves the
# product in the same class: (a, b) * (c, d) ~ (a', b') * (c, d), cross-multiplied (a*c)*(b'*d) = (a'*c)*(b*d). Both
# sides expand so the relation factor a*b' (resp. a'*b) is exposed times c*d.

# The left product expands: (a*c)*(b'*d) = (a*b')*(c*d). A ring identity.
rule multiply-respects-expand-left
  seat a, like integer
  seat b-prime, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a
          read c
        call multiply
          read b-prime
          read d
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a
          read b-prime
        call multiply
          read c
          read d

# The right product expands: (a'*c)*(b*d) = (a'*b)*(c*d). A ring identity.
rule multiply-respects-expand-right
  seat a-prime, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a-prime
          read c
        call multiply
          read b
          read d
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a-prime
          read b
        call multiply
          read c
          read d

# The congruence step for multiplication: under a*b' = a'*b, (a*b')*(c*d) = (a'*b)*(c*d). With the two expansion lemmas
# this proves multiplication respects the relation.
rule multiply-respects-relation-core
  seat a, like integer
  seat a-prime, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat b-prime, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  have h
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        read a
        read b-prime
      call multiply
        read a-prime
        read b
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a
          read b-prime
        call multiply
          read c
          read d
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a-prime
          read b
        call multiply
          read c
          read d
  calm hold

# --- Commutativity, descending from the integers. ---

# Rational addition is commutative: the cross-multiplication of (a, b) + (c, d) against (c, d) + (a, b) is an equality of
# integer polynomials, true for all integers. The two sums are (a*d + b*c, b*d) and (c*b + d*a, d*b), and their
# cross-product (a*d + b*c)*(d*b) = (c*b + d*a)*(b*d) holds by the commutative-ring normalizer. So the two orders land in
# the same class, descending integer commutativity to the rationals.
rule add-is-commutative
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            read d
          call multiply
            read b
            read c
        call multiply
          read d
          read b
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read c
            read b
          call multiply
            read d
            read a
        call multiply
          read b
          read d

# Rational multiplication is commutative: (a, b) * (c, d) and (c, d) * (a, b) cross-multiply equal,
# (a*c)*(d*b) = (c*a)*(b*d), a ring identity. The product class does not depend on the order.
rule multiply-is-commutative
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  seat c, like integer
  seat d, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read a
          read c
        call multiply
          read d
          read b
      call multiply
        call multiply
          read c
          read a
        call multiply
          read b
          read d

# --- Zero is the additive identity up to the relation. ---

# Zero is 0/1. Adding it to (a, b) gives (a*1 + b*0, b*1) = (a, b) up to the relation: the cross-product
# (a*1 + b*0)*b = a*(b*1) holds for all integers (it reduces to a*b = a*b). So 0/1 is the additive identity on classes,
# the neutral element of rational addition.
rule zero-is-additive-identity
  seat a, like integer
  seat b, like integer
  show hold
    call is-equal
      call multiply
        call add
          call multiply
            read a
            code 1
          call multiply
            read b
            code 0
        read b
      call multiply
        read a
        call multiply
          read b
          code 1
